Existing System Analysis
1) Existing System
In the existing agricultural weed-management systems, weed control is largely performed using manual inspection and conventional spraying. Farmers/workers visually inspect crop fields to identify weed patches and then apply pesticides using handheld or uniform field-spraying methods. In some cases, drones are used only for general field monitoring, such as capturing images for inspection, but they typically do not perform full automation such as real-time weed detection + GPS-based weed localization + route planning + automated targeted spraying.

Even when drone-based monitoring exists, most systems rely on post-processing or provide only basic information. The decision of where to spray is still manual, and pesticides may still be applied in a non-targeted manner (uniform or semi-uniform spraying over an area).

2) Limitations of the Existing System (At least 3)
a) Manual Weed Identification (Time-consuming & labour intensive)
Manual weed detection is slow and requires continuous human effort. For large farms, inspection becomes inaccurate due to fatigue and inconsistent observation.

b) Excessive / Non-targeted Pesticide Usage
Conventional spraying often treats the entire area rather than only weed-affected regions. This leads to unnecessary chemical usage, higher cost, and possible environmental damage.

c) Lack of Real-Time Detection and Automated Action
Many existing systems do not detect weeds and trigger spraying immediately. Detection may occur after the field scan, and pesticide application is done separately, increasing operational delay.

d) Limited Accuracy in Weed Localization
When weeds are detected visually, exact coordinates of weed patches may not be captured. Even with drone monitoring, many systems lack precise GPS mapping for spray-target points.

e) No Effective Route Optimization
Existing systems do not calculate the best travel path based on weed locations. As a result, the drone may waste time and energy covering unnecessary areas.

f) Limited Multi-Drone Coordination
Most existing solutions are designed for a single drone or do not coordinate multiple drones effectively. This reduces coverage efficiency and increases completion time.

3) Challenges in the Existing System
The major challenges include:

	* Accurate weed detection under changing lighting, soil background, and crop similarity
	* Efficient coverage of large agricultural fields
	* Reducing unnecessary pesticide application while maintaining weed control effectiveness
	* Translating detected weeds into precise target locations using GPS
	* Reliable drone communication for continuous monitoring and control
	* Planning efficient drone movement paths and synchronized operations (especially for multiple drones)

4) Justification / Need for the Proposed Solution
Because the existing methods are mostly manual and non-targeted, there is a clear need for an automated precision-based system. The proposed system addresses these issues by integrating:

	* Real-time weed detection (YOLO-based model) from drone camera input
	* GPS-based weed localization to identify exact coordinates for spraying
	* Route optimization to reduce flight time and improve coverage efficiency
	* Automated targeted spraying (spray actuator activates only at weed points)
	* Optional multi-drone coordination to scale operations and reduce total completion time

5) Comparison Summary (Existing vs Proposed)
| Feature | Existing System | Proposed System |
|---|---|---|
| Weed detection | Manual/after inspection | Real-time AI detection (YOLO) |
| Spraying approach | Uniform/non-targeted | Targeted spot spraying |
| Labor requirement | High | Reduced |
| Decision time | Delayed | Immediate detection-to-action |
| Localization | Limited accuracy | GPS-guided precise targeting |
| Efficiency | Low due to random coverage | Route optimization for reduced movement |
| Scalability | Often single-drone | Multi-drone support possible |

Conclusion
Overall, the existing agricultural weed management systems suffer from manual dependency, excessive pesticide usage, lack of real-time automation, and weak targeting efficiency. Hence, an AI-enabled precision drone spraying solution is necessary to achieve accurate weed control, lower chemical consumption, reduced labour cost, and improved operational efficiency.
